! conclusion: Wire structural tension as a candidate source that generates actions, not merely as a weight that ranks candidates generated elsewhere.

  + context: War Room decision WR-19 (2026-05-31). Operator: Joe. A live geometric stack should always give the War Machine something to do.

  + IF:
    The geometric stack detects high-tension regions that should drive the War Machine's next action.

  + HOWEVER:
    The current wiring, ΔT as a sorry-only tie-breaker, cannot generate a move; it only ranks an existing menu, so a manifold full of tension still yields nothing when the proposer registries are dry.

  + THEN:
    Add a tension-proposer: an S1 source that emits candidate actions at high-tension regions of the manifold, so the field drives the head. Make this the proximate fix to starvation and the first aif2 build slice.

  + BECAUSE:
    A ranking signal cannot create an option that is absent from the menu. Tension becomes actionable only when it can propose the candidate whose priority it would otherwise merely increase.

    + evidence: The first outing exhausted its proposer-fed queue into learn-action-class before any outward open-mission surfaced; tracing futon2.report.war-machine/judge found structural pressure applied only after four fixed proposers had supplied candidates, and only to address-sorry candidates.
